---
title: "A longer read"
toc: true
---

This page is deliberately long. It exists to be scrolled, so the point is orientation: how a
reader keeps their bearings on a page with no chapter breaks to lean on.

## On length

A long document is not a failure of editing; sometimes the subject is large. What a long
document owes its reader is orientation. A printed book gives it for free: the heft in your
left hand versus your right tells you, without a word, how far you have come and how far is
left. A web page has one answer already built in, the scrollbar, and it is a coarse one:
it measures pixels rather than ideas. But it is *there*, on every page, in the shape every
reader already knows, and a second bar painted along the top says the same coarse thing
twice while moving in the corner of your eye. So this page adds nothing to it. What is worth
adding is the part the scrollbar genuinely cannot do, which is the next two sections.

## On the table of contents

The second thing a long read owes its reader is a live answer to "where am I", not just a
scrollbar. With `toc: true`, the section you are in highlights in the table of contents as you
scroll, so a glance at the rail says where you are without you doing the arithmetic yourself.
That is the whole trick: no memory of a past visit required, just an honest, current answer
every time you look up.

## On estimates

People ask "how long will this take?" before they ask "is this any good?". An honest estimate,
even a rough one, is a kindness: it lets the reader choose the right moment. Reading speed
varies, so the number is approximate by nature, and that is fine. Counting the prose and
ignoring the code and the equations gives a figure close enough to be useful and never
pretends to a precision it does not have.

## On restraint

The danger with reading aids is that they crowd the page. A progress bar that pulses, a
counter that jumps: each one steals attention from the words, which is the opposite of the
point. The right amount of chrome is the least amount that still answers the reader's quiet
question, *where am I*, and then gets out of the way. The browser's own scrollbar already
answers it, which is why there is no progress bar here to answer it twice, and the table of
contents above answers it a second, more specific way: which section, not just how far down
the pixels run.

## A middle section

Here is more prose so the page is genuinely long. The measure of a reading surface is not how
much it can show but how little it asks. Every control is a small tax on attention. The ones
worth keeping are the ones a reader would miss if they were gone, and would not think about
while they were there. Orientation passes that test: it is invisible until needed and obvious
the moment it is.

## On finishing

A reader who reaches the end of a long piece has given you something scarce, their sustained
attention, and the least you can do is make the journey legible while it happens. By the time
these words are on screen the scrollbar's thumb should be resting at the bottom of its track
and the estimate near zero. The end of a document is a small event worth marking quietly, not
with confetti, just with the honest absence of anywhere further to go.

## The end

There is nothing after this heading except the satisfaction of having arrived, and the table
of contents above should agree: this last section, highlighted, with nowhere further to go.
